From 047fc3e29a926787e21ac2bf245859a2cf3ed186 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ElXreno <elxreno@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 01:56:17 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] abd: keep clean ARC buffers as folios in the page cache

A scatter ABD's chunks are folios from allocation. Once the buffer holding
them is final, hashed and with no io on it, they are added to the page cache
of one anonymous inode, so the kernel ages them next to everything else it
caches, takes them back through release_folio when memory is short and moves
them through migrate_folio when it compacts. The abd_t sits in each folio's
private word; the mapping owns one reference per page and the private word
one more, and anyone who needs a page for a while, an iterator or a bio,
takes a reference through abd_sg_grab(), which is what stops a chunk from
being moved or reclaimed underneath them: both kernel paths refuse a folio
whose count is not exactly what they expect.

Reclaim reaches the owner through a pair of callbacks: lock it, then ask it
to give the buffer up. The ARC answers with the hash lock and arc_evict_hdr(),
after the same checks the eviction thread makes. Its free path recognises
the folio the reclaimer holds locked and only detaches it, leaving removal
and freeing to the kernel. Locks nest owner, abd_mtx, folio; reclaim and
migration arrive holding the folio lock and only ever try the other two.

Joined buffers are read only until freed, which is what makes the copy in
migration trivially consistent; the writers in abd.c assert it. Clean bytes
held this way are no longer offered to the ARC shrinker, since the LRU
already has them. The single chunk linear shortcut is gone on this backend
because a cached address would go stale when the folio moved.

Needs filemap_add_folio and filemap_remove_folio exported to a CDDL module;
configure falls back to the stock backend where they are not.
